1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,089
I told you it'd work!

2
00:00:02,140 --> 00:00:03,970
It very nearly ate you for dinner.

3
00:00:04,019 --> 00:00:06,450
Oh, admit it. I totally saved your life.

4
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:08,699
It wasn't going to eat me.

5
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:10,890
I totally saved you from

6
00:00:10,939 --> 00:00:13,810
having to marry that giant
sentient plant thing.

7
00:00:13,859 --> 00:00:17,260
That bit where I jumped over
the side? That was amazing!

8
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:20,329
Hah! I knew you were impressed!

9
00:00:20,379 --> 00:00:23,129
The second most beautiful
garden in all of time and space,

10
00:00:23,179 --> 00:00:26,289
and we can never come back here
because you, Miss Oswald, decided...

11
00:00:45,539 --> 00:00:46,689
Hello?

12
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:48,850
'Clara? Finally. It's Rigsy.'

13
00:00:48,899 --> 00:00:51,329
Oh. Rigsy. Hey. What's wrong?

14
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:53,420
So I have this, er...

15
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:56,899
It kind of looks like a tattoo.

16
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:02,009
'Seriously? I gave you this
number for emergencies.'

17
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:04,810
It's an emergency, trust me. Just...

18
00:01:04,859 --> 00:01:07,049
'Come and take a look at it. Please.'

19
00:01:07,099 --> 00:01:09,329
Who said you could give out my number?

20
00:01:09,379 --> 00:01:11,409
Look, look, no matter how bad it is,

21
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:15,219
we cannot take you back down your
timeline just to fix a tattoo.

22
00:01:16,299 --> 00:01:19,569
That's just it. I didn't
get a tattoo. And it's...

23
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:21,609
It's counting down.

24
00:01:21,659 --> 00:01:23,250
Sorry, what?

25
00:01:23,299 --> 00:01:27,049
The tattoo... it's a number and
it's counting down to zero.

26
00:01:27,099 --> 00:01:28,969
Hang tight. We'll be right there.

27
00:01:29,019 --> 00:01:30,739
Hurry. Please.

28
00:02:16,659 --> 00:02:20,159
- Did you make this human?
- Lucy? Yeah, she's mine.

29
00:02:21,699 --> 00:02:25,050
Hello. Oh, Rigsy, she's gorgeous.

30
00:02:25,099 --> 00:02:28,810
She's better than that. She's brilliant.

31
00:02:28,860 --> 00:02:32,460
- What are you doing running round getting tattoos when there's...
- Shh!

32
00:02:34,099 --> 00:02:35,889
Look, I didn't "get" anything.

33
00:02:35,939 --> 00:02:38,969
I woke up this morning and it
was just there. Jen noticed it.

34
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:42,020
OK, show me this tattoo
you didn't get, then.

35
00:02:45,659 --> 00:02:47,449
It's a tattoo. It's very boring.

36
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:49,689
No, wait. Just keep watching.

37
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:51,490
What were you doing last night?

38
00:02:51,539 --> 00:02:54,539
That's just it... yesterday
was a total blank.

39
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:59,009
Jen said I left the house
before dawn, I missed work,

40
00:02:59,060 --> 00:03:02,620
and I didn't get back till after
midnight. No-one saw me all day.

41
00:03:05,099 --> 00:03:07,099
Oh, that's not boring.

42
00:03:08,659 --> 00:03:10,979
That is very not boring.

43
00:03:12,979 --> 00:03:15,449
What? What is it?

44
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:19,780
OK, Local Knowledge, you're coming
with us. Bring the new human.

45
00:03:20,819 --> 00:03:24,620
No! Don't bring the new human.
I'll just get distracted.

46
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,329
If you want your extremities
to stay attached,

47
00:03:30,379 --> 00:03:31,729
stand absolutely still.

48
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:35,300
If not, we can provide a small bag,
you can take them home at the end.

49
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:38,259
Rigsy, your phone.

50
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:41,889
It's like they've wiped
it, but only the last day.

51
00:03:41,939 --> 00:03:45,409
No location data, no texts, nothing.

52
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:48,409
You're sure the screen wasn't
cracked before yesterday?

53
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:49,490
Mm-hm.

54
00:03:49,539 --> 00:03:53,009
Oh, right, OK, here we go. Ah...

55
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:54,729
Good.

56
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:56,050
Weird.

57
00:03:56,099 --> 00:03:57,329
Good and weird.

58
00:03:57,379 --> 00:03:58,569
Can I...?

59
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:01,259
Oh, yes, yes. Of course.

60
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:04,560
First off...

61
00:04:05,379 --> 00:04:06,770
in the last 24 hours,

62
00:04:06,819 --> 00:04:09,770
you've had significant
contact with alien life-forms,

63
00:04:09,819 --> 00:04:11,770
right here in the centre of London.

64
00:04:11,819 --> 00:04:14,770
OK, so why don't I remember anything?

65
00:04:14,819 --> 00:04:17,370
- You've been retconned.
- Huh? - What-conned?

66
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:21,379
Amnesia drug. Your pre-frontal
cortex is marinating in it.

67
00:04:22,899 --> 00:04:25,100
Oh, there's something else!

68
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:27,279
Something...

69
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:32,129
Er...

70
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:33,930
not good.

71
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:35,379
Weird.

72
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:52,240
What's he doing?

73
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:56,329
He's making an effort to be nice.

74
00:04:56,379 --> 00:04:59,490
There is no nice way to
say you're about to die.

75
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:00,819
What?!

76
00:05:05,779 --> 00:05:09,329
- Rigsy...
- No, no, no, no, don't start using my actual name now!

77
00:05:09,379 --> 00:05:12,089
Call me Pudding Brain, call
me Local Knowledge, whatever.

78
00:05:12,139 --> 00:05:13,930
Just don't call me Rigsy.

79
00:05:13,980 --> 00:05:17,860
You're going to save me. You're
a doctor. That's what you do.

80
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:30,170
OK. OK...

81
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:33,290
Yes, OK, let's do this thing.

82
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:35,730
First up, stop the countdown.

83
00:05:35,779 --> 00:05:39,090
526 minutes, right! OK.

84
00:05:39,139 --> 00:05:43,529
Yes, you know what, Local Knowledge,
I don't know who did this to you

85
00:05:43,579 --> 00:05:46,449
or why. But I do almost certainly know...

86
00:05:47,459 --> 00:05:49,399
how to find them.

87
00:05:57,659 --> 00:05:59,129
There have always been rumours.

88
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:01,449
Stories passed from
traveller to traveller,

89
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:03,569
mutterings about hidden streets,

90
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:06,050
secret pockets of alien life

91
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:07,850
right here on Earth.

92
00:06:07,899 --> 00:06:11,529
Like a smuggler's cove, only not
a cove, because it's right here.

93
00:06:11,579 --> 00:06:13,250
Right in the middle of the capital.

94
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:15,930
- The hidden places are in the Great British Library?
- No.

95
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:17,579
The maps are.

96
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:20,889
I never put stock in it.

97
00:06:20,939 --> 00:06:24,290
London streets that suddenly
disappeared from human view? No.

98
00:06:24,339 --> 00:06:27,250
You lot are always overlooking
things, but whole streets?

99
00:06:27,300 --> 00:06:29,610
That would be excessive, even for you.

100
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:32,490
If the stories are true,
though, there should be a street

101
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:35,850
on one of these old maps that no
longer exists in the real world.

102
00:06:35,899 --> 00:06:38,240
Like a trap street, only not.

103
00:06:38,980 --> 00:06:41,610
- What did you say?
- A trap street.

104
00:06:41,659 --> 00:06:44,930
You know, when someone's making
a map, a, um... cartographer,

105
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:46,970
uses a fake street,
throws it into the mix,

106
00:06:47,019 --> 00:06:50,209
names it after one of his kids or
whatever. Then if the fake street,

107
00:06:50,259 --> 00:06:52,610
the trap street, ever shows
up on someone else's map,

108
00:06:52,659 --> 00:06:55,009
they know their work's
been stolen. Clever, right?

109
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:57,610
My God. A whole London
street just up and disappeared

110
00:06:57,659 --> 00:06:59,889
and you lot assume it's
a copyright infringement.

111
00:06:59,939 --> 00:07:02,009
So we're looking for a trap street?

112
00:07:02,060 --> 00:07:05,050
We're looking for a trap street and
we're not going to find it here.

113
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:07,649
The glasses are tracking
your eye movements.

114
00:07:07,699 --> 00:07:10,009
Just keep looking straight down and...

115
00:07:10,060 --> 00:07:13,860
I know! Focus on the
buildings directly below me.

116
00:07:15,699 --> 00:07:19,449
Whatever they're using, it
only hides the street itself.

117
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:22,850
It prevents you from noticing
there's even something missing.

118
00:07:22,899 --> 00:07:25,610
They're somehow making our
eyes skate right over it.

119
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:28,329
Let's call it a misdirection circuit.

120
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:35,250
Clara!

121
00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:43,779
Hello, London!

122
00:07:52,699 --> 00:07:54,100
I'm good.

123
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:56,610
I'm good.

124
00:07:56,659 --> 00:07:59,810
She enjoyed that... way too much.

125
00:07:59,860 --> 00:08:03,339
Tell me about it. It's
an ongoing problem. Here.

126
00:08:04,779 --> 00:08:06,449
Keep it steady.

127
00:08:07,379 --> 00:08:10,449
Just move it slowly over the grid.
When we're done, we'll have a map

128
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:14,100
of the areas of the grid
that Clara couldn't focus on.

129
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:18,540
So, these are the bits
my eyes skated over.

130
00:08:19,579 --> 00:08:22,610
OK, we split up. Clara, that way.

131
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:24,459
Local Knowledge...

132
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:28,610
Forget the way you
usually look at the world.

133
00:08:28,660 --> 00:08:31,290
This street's going to
be hiding in plain sight.

134
00:08:31,339 --> 00:08:35,049
- 22, 23...
- If you see something unusual or notable,

135
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:37,440
dismiss it. Just keep walking.

136
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:41,450
But if there's a bit of
London so unremarkable

137
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:43,690
that you don't even think about it...

138
00:08:43,740 --> 00:08:45,250
stop.

139
00:08:45,299 --> 00:08:48,929
You could very well be standing
right outside a trap street.

140
00:08:48,980 --> 00:08:52,850
- Count everything that you see.
- Four, five, six...

141
00:08:52,899 --> 00:08:55,529
Because when you hit the
area around a trap street,

142
00:08:55,580 --> 00:08:58,179
it's very likely you'll lose count.

143
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:01,730
79...

144
00:09:01,779 --> 00:09:03,450
80...

145
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:04,700
81...

146
00:09:05,980 --> 00:09:07,370
82.

147
00:09:07,419 --> 00:09:09,970
- Huh?
- Remember... 82.

148
00:09:10,019 --> 00:09:11,960
- Come along!
- 82!

149
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:16,850
You'll lose count because
the misdirection circuit

150
00:09:16,899 --> 00:09:19,330
is creating confusion in your mind.

151
00:09:19,379 --> 00:09:24,129
Details won't add up. Reality
will have glitches in it.

152
00:09:24,179 --> 00:09:27,809
Like when you try to read the same
simple sentence three times over...

153
00:09:27,860 --> 00:09:29,490
One, two, three...

154
00:09:29,539 --> 00:09:32,279
and the meaning just won't sink in.

155
00:09:35,580 --> 00:09:36,919
Got ya.

156
00:09:43,059 --> 00:09:45,250
Clara! Clara!

157
00:09:45,299 --> 00:09:46,570
Clara!

158
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,409
It's off this street, I am certain.

159
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:53,409
We're very close.

160
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:55,370
We need to distract our other senses.

161
00:09:55,419 --> 00:09:59,179
Clara, go back to the TARDIS.
Pick up all my most annoying stuff.

162
00:10:16,259 --> 00:10:19,289
- What happened to the stuff I asked you to bring?
- Someone called you.

163
00:10:19,340 --> 00:10:21,740
Yesterday, 6am. Blocked number.

164
00:10:31,620 --> 00:10:33,090
She's dead.

165
00:10:34,379 --> 00:10:37,340
What is it? What are you remembering?

166
00:10:42,980 --> 00:10:44,779
Rigsy, what is it?

167
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:50,179
You can't see it? There!

168
00:11:05,139 --> 00:11:06,809
I see it. You?

169
00:11:08,580 --> 00:11:10,320
50 minutes left.

170
00:11:11,179 --> 00:11:15,340
Hoodie up, Local Knowledge. They
know what you look like in there.

171
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:29,330
How come I saw it when you guys couldn't?

172
00:11:29,379 --> 00:11:31,250
You were upset, weren't you?

173
00:11:31,299 --> 00:11:33,570
Something slipped through
the retcon memory,

174
00:11:33,620 --> 00:11:38,129
something that took over your
whole mind, something juicy.

175
00:11:38,179 --> 00:11:41,529
So the misdirection circuit
lost its power over you.

176
00:11:41,580 --> 00:11:44,490
Surely people wander in here
all the time, then, distracted,

177
00:11:44,539 --> 00:11:47,940
- on their phones or whatever?
- Perhaps they do...

178
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:09,059
Three at once. That's new.

179
00:12:11,419 --> 00:12:12,450
Hang about.

180
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:15,700
This one don't smell human.

181
00:12:16,779 --> 00:12:19,570
Name, species and case for
asylum. Quick as you like.

182
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:23,460
- Asylum?
- The reason you're here. The reason you need sanctuary.

183
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:27,769
Why didn't they use the protocol?

184
00:12:27,820 --> 00:12:29,970
I saw through the circuit
again. I saw them.

185
00:12:30,019 --> 00:12:31,889
They're definitely not human.

186
00:12:31,940 --> 00:12:34,779
You do know this is a refugee camp?

187
00:12:37,659 --> 00:12:39,289
Yeah, of course.

188
00:12:39,340 --> 00:12:44,460
- Of course he does! Now that you've told him.
- Mayor Me.

189
00:12:46,019 --> 00:12:48,169
- Ashildr!
- Ashildr?

190
00:12:48,220 --> 00:12:51,929
That's your name. I keep telling you that.

191
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:53,250
Do you?

192
00:12:53,299 --> 00:12:58,970
Infinite lifespan, finite memory...
It makes for an awkward social life.

193
00:12:59,019 --> 00:13:00,649
You must be Clara Oswald.

194
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:03,299
You're as beautiful as your photos.

195
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:06,090
We met.

196
00:13:06,139 --> 00:13:07,929
Yes, I know. It's in my diaries.

197
00:13:07,980 --> 00:13:11,210
Oh, don't look like that! I
enjoyed our conversations.

198
00:13:11,259 --> 00:13:13,129
I've read them many times.

199
00:13:13,179 --> 00:13:16,129
OK, that's... slightly odd. But nice.

200
00:13:16,179 --> 00:13:17,450
Um, hang on,

201
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:21,529
so this is where you've been.
That's why he lost track of you.

202
00:13:21,580 --> 00:13:25,529
Oh, come on, please. It's really
cute, he thinks I don't know.

203
00:13:25,580 --> 00:13:27,649
He's got this whole
secret room in the TARDIS

204
00:13:27,700 --> 00:13:31,299
- where he collects mentions of you.
- It's not cute.

205
00:13:32,019 --> 00:13:33,490
It's surveillance.

206
00:13:33,539 --> 00:13:36,409
- It's professional interest.
- Precautionary measure.

207
00:13:36,460 --> 00:13:38,490
Still saving the world from me, then?

208
00:13:38,539 --> 00:13:40,330
It's still here, isn't it?

209
00:13:40,379 --> 00:13:43,009
He lost track of you in the early 1800s.

210
00:13:43,059 --> 00:13:46,620
- I wondered if you were...
- Oh, no. I let him know I was OK.

211
00:13:49,379 --> 00:13:50,929
I saw you.

212
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:53,090
No. I got your attention.

213
00:13:53,139 --> 00:13:56,210
Yes, you did, and you have.
Now we need your help.

214
00:13:56,259 --> 00:14:00,500
Someone in this place is in
control of a Quantum Shade.

215
00:14:07,100 --> 00:14:10,250
- I knew I recognised that smell.
- Oh.

216
00:14:10,299 --> 00:14:12,779
Ashildr? What's going on?

217
00:14:25,379 --> 00:14:26,610
You.

218
00:14:26,659 --> 00:14:28,850
How do you know this man?

219
00:14:28,899 --> 00:14:31,299
Hang on. You did this to Rigsy?

220
00:14:32,220 --> 00:14:33,730
What have you done?

221
00:14:33,779 --> 00:14:36,460
This man committed a crime.

222
00:14:38,539 --> 00:14:40,450
- I sentenced him.
- Sentenced him?

223
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:42,970
I also gave him enough time to return home

224
00:14:43,019 --> 00:14:44,490
and say goodbye to his family.

225
00:14:44,539 --> 00:14:46,129
You flooded his brain with retcon!

226
00:14:46,179 --> 00:14:48,889
Till we showed up, he didn't
even know he had to say goodbye.

227
00:14:48,940 --> 00:14:52,090
I'm afraid no intruder leaves
this place without a memory wipe.

228
00:14:52,139 --> 00:14:54,330
With respect, that will include you.

229
00:14:54,379 --> 00:14:55,850
Oh, the hell it will!

230
00:14:55,899 --> 00:14:59,450
Ashildr, given we're all going to
forget this conversation anyway,

231
00:14:59,500 --> 00:15:02,049
perhaps you could tell us
what happened here yesterday

232
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:04,570
to necessitate a death sentence?

233
00:15:05,580 --> 00:15:07,740
Fine, I'll show you.

234
00:15:09,419 --> 00:15:11,929
Mr Kabel, Mr Rump.

235
00:15:11,980 --> 00:15:13,450
Permit them entry.

236
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:14,889
No!

237
00:15:14,940 --> 00:15:17,730
You've already endangered
one of my friends.

238
00:15:17,779 --> 00:15:21,649
I want your personal guarantee
you will not endanger another.

239
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:24,039
Shut up, I can handle myself.

240
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:27,690
I guarantee the safety of Clara Oswald.

241
00:15:27,740 --> 00:15:32,409
She will be under my personal
protection. That is absolute.

242
00:15:32,460 --> 00:15:34,799
If that's your wish, Mayor Me.

243
00:15:39,179 --> 00:15:41,529
This way.

244
00:15:41,580 --> 00:15:43,289
Murderer.

245
00:15:43,340 --> 00:15:45,139
What did you say?

246
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:53,899
Murderer.

247
00:15:55,259 --> 00:15:57,330
He called me a murderer.

248
00:16:02,379 --> 00:16:04,769
So you're still calling yourself Me, then?

249
00:16:04,820 --> 00:16:06,649
- Me?
- Mayor Me.

250
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:09,730
Mayor is a title. I give myself a
title for the same reason you do,

251
00:16:09,779 --> 00:16:12,850
- Doctor... something to live up to.
- Difficult, isn't it?

252
00:16:12,899 --> 00:16:15,730
- How long have you been here?
- Since Waterloo.

253
00:16:15,779 --> 00:16:18,929
- The battle?
- No, the station. Really, Doctor.

254
00:16:18,980 --> 00:16:20,929
Tread carefully while you're here.

255
00:16:20,980 --> 00:16:23,970
Some of your greatest enemies
are within a few feet of you.

256
00:16:24,019 --> 00:16:27,289
As far as you're concerned, this is
the most dangerous street in London.

257
00:16:27,340 --> 00:16:31,210
Fascinating. Now, can we skip to the part
where you want Rigsy dead for some reason?

258
00:16:31,259 --> 00:16:33,200
It's him! He's back!

259
00:16:34,299 --> 00:16:36,409
It's best we get him inside first.

260
00:16:36,460 --> 00:16:38,450
Murderer. You're not welcome here.

261
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:41,610
They look at me as if they
want to kill me themselves.

262
00:16:41,659 --> 00:16:44,690
- Don't want your kind round here.
- Murderer!

263
00:16:48,539 --> 00:16:51,210
Like I said, it's best we get inside.

264
00:16:55,460 --> 00:16:57,200
Filthy murderer!

265
00:17:05,539 --> 00:17:07,329
Wait, Clara.

266
00:17:07,380 --> 00:17:08,579
Look.

267
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:17,579
This misdirection circuit
of yours is remarkable.

268
00:17:18,859 --> 00:17:21,650
The cloaking device that hides the street,

269
00:17:21,700 --> 00:17:23,890
makes everyone look like humans.

270
00:17:23,940 --> 00:17:25,529
It's no device.

271
00:17:25,579 --> 00:17:27,450
It's the Lurkworms.

272
00:17:28,660 --> 00:17:30,329
Quite something, aren't they?

273
00:17:30,380 --> 00:17:32,930
The light is a telepathic field.

274
00:17:32,980 --> 00:17:35,450
It normalises everything you see,

275
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:40,019
places it within the compass of
your expectations, your experiences.

276
00:17:42,779 --> 00:17:45,180
You can bypass them, of course.

277
00:17:50,019 --> 00:17:51,259
Ow!

278
00:17:57,059 --> 00:17:59,599
Don't worry. We're perfectly safe.

279
00:18:00,180 --> 00:18:02,569
Yes, a phrase I find
is usually followed by

280
00:18:02,619 --> 00:18:05,170
a lot of screaming and
running and bleeding.

281
00:18:05,220 --> 00:18:10,130
I brokered a truce. We have strict
rules against violence here.

282
00:18:10,180 --> 00:18:11,769
Rules every creature must abide by

283
00:18:11,819 --> 00:18:13,609
if they wish to remain on the street.

284
00:18:13,660 --> 00:18:16,170
- Get away from us!
- Don't want your kind round here.

285
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:20,049
What's better, that they're in here
with me, peaceful and cooperative,

286
00:18:20,099 --> 00:18:22,289
or out there on Earth like the Zygons?

287
00:18:22,339 --> 00:18:25,329
We haven't had an act of violence
on this street for 100 years,

288
00:18:25,380 --> 00:18:28,049
until yesterday, when
your friend here attacked

289
00:18:28,099 --> 00:18:30,329
one of our most vulnerable residents.

290
00:18:30,380 --> 00:18:32,579
How did Rigsy even get in?

291
00:18:34,059 --> 00:18:37,900
I mean, we barely managed it, and
we knew what we were looking for...

292
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:43,180
She was found at the
entrance of the street.

293
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:46,289
No weapon on the scene,

294
00:18:46,339 --> 00:18:48,930
but the cause of death
is likely the head wound.

295
00:18:48,980 --> 00:18:51,849
- Seems she was knocked to
the cobblestones. - "Seems"?

296
00:18:51,900 --> 00:18:54,049
You've sentenced Rigsy to death

297
00:18:54,099 --> 00:18:56,650
yet you don't know
exactly what's going on?

298
00:18:56,700 --> 00:18:58,690
He was found over the body.

299
00:18:58,740 --> 00:19:02,089
My people were angry,
frightened. I had to act.

300
00:19:02,140 --> 00:19:04,569
- This is ridiculous, this is...
- What was her name?

301
00:19:04,619 --> 00:19:06,089
Anah.

302
00:19:06,140 --> 00:19:09,700
We're keeping her here until someone
can take her home for burial.

303
00:19:11,059 --> 00:19:12,730
She's a Janus!

304
00:19:13,660 --> 00:19:17,170
She escaped slavery. She
fled here with her child.

305
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:19,960
- The child. A daughter?
- No. A boy.

306
00:19:20,619 --> 00:19:21,849
Is that bad?

307
00:19:21,900 --> 00:19:24,250
No, it's not bad, it's just unhelpful.

308
00:19:24,299 --> 00:19:27,299
A daughter might've seen
who killed her mother.

309
00:19:30,539 --> 00:19:33,009
The female Janus is psychic.

310
00:19:33,059 --> 00:19:35,089
One face sees into the future,

311
00:19:35,140 --> 00:19:37,769
the other looks behind her, into the past.

312
00:19:37,819 --> 00:19:40,049
I think we saw her son outside.

313
00:19:40,099 --> 00:19:43,089
Clara, what if I did do it?

314
00:19:43,140 --> 00:19:47,049
I mean, I wouldn't have
meant to hurt her, but...

315
00:19:47,099 --> 00:19:49,569
What if I wandered in and saw
what she really looked like?

316
00:19:49,619 --> 00:19:51,009
What if I freaked?

317
00:19:51,059 --> 00:19:53,369
You didn't just wander in here.

318
00:19:53,420 --> 00:19:57,130
You were called here at 6am by
a number from a mystery phone.

319
00:19:57,180 --> 00:19:59,730
There is no way you did this.

320
00:19:59,779 --> 00:20:03,930
So, what then? You think someone
called him here? Set him up?

321
00:20:03,980 --> 00:20:05,809
- Yes!
- [Mayor!]

322
00:20:05,859 --> 00:20:07,369
Obviously! Which means

323
00:20:07,420 --> 00:20:10,009
one of your pet aliens out
there is the real killer.

324
00:20:10,059 --> 00:20:13,690
- I just need to talk to her.
- Excuse me. I'm sorry.

325
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:17,609
Yes. Please, go. It's not like we've
got a ticking clock or anything.

326
00:20:17,660 --> 00:20:19,529
Mayor, I beg of you, please...

327
00:20:19,579 --> 00:20:20,849
41 minutes.

328
00:20:20,900 --> 00:20:25,609
Lock me up, throw us
out, anything but this.

329
00:20:25,660 --> 00:20:28,819
Please. I only took it to save her.

330
00:20:30,380 --> 00:20:32,380
How many minutes left?

331
00:20:35,099 --> 00:20:36,900
Two, Madam Mayor.

332
00:20:42,180 --> 00:20:45,210
This man stole medical rations.

333
00:20:45,259 --> 00:20:49,170
He broke a rule of the street
and he stole from all of you.

334
00:20:49,220 --> 00:20:52,740
And yes, I can remove the chronolock.

335
00:20:54,180 --> 00:20:55,720
But I won't.

336
00:20:56,700 --> 00:20:59,140
Our rules keep us safe.

337
00:21:01,900 --> 00:21:03,569
Give it to me.

338
00:21:04,420 --> 00:21:06,490
Please.

339
00:21:06,539 --> 00:21:09,650
Tell me I can have it. One word. Say it.

340
00:21:09,700 --> 00:21:11,849
Say yes.

341
00:21:11,900 --> 00:21:15,980
I did this to save you,
you silly old thing.

342
00:21:17,420 --> 00:21:20,180
You really think I could lose you now?

343
00:21:26,059 --> 00:21:28,130
What's happening?

344
00:21:28,180 --> 00:21:30,450
It's called a Quantum Shade.
It's kind of a spirit.

345
00:21:30,500 --> 00:21:33,250
Once it's bound to a victim,

346
00:21:33,299 --> 00:21:37,250
you could flee across all of
time and all of the universe,

347
00:21:37,299 --> 00:21:39,369
it would still find you.

348
00:21:47,859 --> 00:21:50,970
Don't run. Stay with her.

349
00:21:56,420 --> 00:21:58,049
Don't go!

350
00:21:58,099 --> 00:22:00,170
Why do they always run?

351
00:22:08,619 --> 00:22:10,490
Help me, somebody, please!

352
00:22:10,539 --> 00:22:13,049
At least give him a merciful death.

353
00:22:13,099 --> 00:22:17,130
Do you think a Cyberman
fears a merciful death?

354
00:22:17,180 --> 00:22:19,170
Help me, please!

355
00:22:19,220 --> 00:22:22,160
Peace on this street depends on one thing.

356
00:22:23,140 --> 00:22:26,980
To break it in any way
is to face the raven.

357
00:22:27,980 --> 00:22:29,650
Please help me!

358
00:23:16,980 --> 00:23:20,339
I have no wish to harm your
friend if he's innocent, Doctor.

359
00:23:22,099 --> 00:23:26,009
Question anyone. Examine the body.

360
00:23:26,059 --> 00:23:29,500
But it's not me you need to
convince of Rigsy's innocence.

361
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:31,970
It's them.

362
00:23:43,339 --> 00:23:45,769
OK, we split up. Cover more ground.

363
00:23:45,819 --> 00:23:47,450
I'm good cop, you're bad cop.

364
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:50,170
No, no, we don't have to...
Can I not be the good cop?

365
00:23:50,220 --> 00:23:53,450
- Doctor, we've discussed this. Your face.
- Oh, yes.

366
00:23:53,500 --> 00:23:56,769
Forget about cops. Forget
about finding the real killer.

367
00:23:56,819 --> 00:24:00,089
You heard Ashildr. All we have
to do is persuade these creatures

368
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:02,480
that it isn't Rigsy. And fast.

369
00:24:04,460 --> 00:24:06,049
Ssh! Ssh!

370
00:24:06,099 --> 00:24:07,769
'She's been like this all day.'

371
00:24:07,819 --> 00:24:10,490
Listen, you be good for your mum, OK?

372
00:24:10,539 --> 00:24:12,730
I'm doing my best to get home to you guys.

373
00:24:12,779 --> 00:24:14,769
- 'She won't stop crying.'
- Yeah, I know.

374
00:24:14,819 --> 00:24:17,690
Yeah, she can probably tell you're upset.

375
00:24:18,539 --> 00:24:20,019
Rump?

376
00:24:21,380 --> 00:24:23,319
It's Rump, isn't it?

377
00:24:24,740 --> 00:24:27,009
That man's wife. She said something.

378
00:24:27,059 --> 00:24:31,210
"Give it to me, tell me I can
have it." What did she mean?

379
00:24:31,259 --> 00:24:33,859
Two ways to survive a Quantum Shade.

380
00:24:34,619 --> 00:24:37,619
The Shade's master
removes the chronolock...

381
00:24:38,380 --> 00:24:41,009
or you can give it to someone else.

382
00:24:41,059 --> 00:24:42,609
Give it? You can just...

383
00:24:42,660 --> 00:24:45,569
No, you can't just push it on
someone. It's not that simple.

384
00:24:45,619 --> 00:24:49,180
It has to be taken willingly.
The death's already locked in.

385
00:24:50,579 --> 00:24:54,420
You can pass it on,
but... you can't cheat it.

386
00:25:12,980 --> 00:25:16,890
You're serious? You actually expect
me to give you my death sentence?

387
00:25:16,940 --> 00:25:20,410
Ssh! Go on, I've always wanted a tattoo.

388
00:25:20,460 --> 00:25:23,490
- You know, something small. Discreet.
- Clara. Cut it out.

389
00:25:23,539 --> 00:25:27,009
Weren't you listening? I'm under
the Mayor's personal protection.

390
00:25:27,059 --> 00:25:30,250
And it's absolute, apparently.
Look, she controls the raven,

391
00:25:30,299 --> 00:25:32,690
so I will never have to
face it. This is clever.

392
00:25:32,740 --> 00:25:34,690
But this is putting you in danger.

393
00:25:34,740 --> 00:25:37,410
No, this is us talking the
opposition into their own trap.

394
00:25:37,460 --> 00:25:39,529
This is Doctor 101. We're buying time.

395
00:25:39,579 --> 00:25:42,130
We get all the aliens on our
side in the next half an hour,

396
00:25:42,180 --> 00:25:45,490
and then we reveal I've got the
chronolock, not you, and boom!

397
00:25:45,539 --> 00:25:47,849
We buy ourselves more time
to find the real killer.

398
00:25:47,900 --> 00:25:49,849
The Doctor would never let you do this.

399
00:25:49,900 --> 00:25:52,170
Doctor 102... never tell
anyone your actual plan.

400
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:54,049
He'll have a tantrum when he finds out.

401
00:25:54,099 --> 00:25:56,250
And then, when we confront Ashildr,

402
00:25:56,299 --> 00:25:59,619
she'll want to take the chronolock
off just to shut him up.

403
00:26:02,460 --> 00:26:05,890
What happens if you don't go
home tonight to Jen and Lucy, eh?

404
00:26:05,940 --> 00:26:07,849
If you never go home?

405
00:26:07,900 --> 00:26:10,930
You really want your little
girl growing up without a father

406
00:26:10,980 --> 00:26:13,650
just because he wouldn't take a risk?

407
00:26:15,099 --> 00:26:18,400
You trusted us to save
you, so trust us. Come on.

408
00:26:23,380 --> 00:26:25,099
OK. All right.

409
00:26:26,660 --> 00:26:28,210
How do we do this, then?

410
00:26:28,259 --> 00:26:30,650
Well, I was kind of
hoping that would be it.

411
00:26:30,700 --> 00:26:33,730
I say I want it, you say,
"You can have it"... done deal.

412
00:26:33,779 --> 00:26:36,119
Hey, turn around, let me see.

413
00:26:55,420 --> 00:26:59,490
So this is your life, then?
Bouncing around time, saving people?

414
00:26:59,539 --> 00:27:01,250
No, not every day.

415
00:27:01,299 --> 00:27:05,009
Sometimes Jane Austen
and I prank each other.

416
00:27:05,059 --> 00:27:07,529
Oh, she is the worst. I love her.

417
00:27:07,940 --> 00:27:10,009
Take that how you like.

418
00:27:12,700 --> 00:27:15,049
Are you sure it wasn't
someone from the street?

419
00:27:15,099 --> 00:27:18,009
I've told you already there wasn't
anyone up that end of the street

420
00:27:18,059 --> 00:27:19,490
except Anah and the human.

421
00:27:19,539 --> 00:27:23,049
I've identified 27 different
species on this street so far,

422
00:27:23,099 --> 00:27:26,049
15 of whom are known for aggression.

423
00:27:26,099 --> 00:27:29,089
Why is it so hard to believe one
of them is capable of murder?

424
00:27:29,140 --> 00:27:33,369
Capable of murder? Yeah.
Capable of killing Anah? No.

425
00:27:33,420 --> 00:27:35,529
Why not? What's so special about her?

426
00:27:35,579 --> 00:27:39,170
It was the way she looked
at you. Like she understood.

427
00:27:39,220 --> 00:27:42,289
One glance into your
past and she felt it all.

428
00:27:42,339 --> 00:27:45,329
Every battle, every loss.

429
00:27:45,380 --> 00:27:47,329
So you just want the
human dead, is that it?

430
00:27:47,380 --> 00:27:48,529
You don't get it, do you?

431
00:27:48,579 --> 00:27:50,809
If the human didn't do it,
that means one of us did it,

432
00:27:50,859 --> 00:27:53,690
which means folks start pointing
fingers, turning on each other,

433
00:27:53,740 --> 00:27:55,849
and once we turn on each
other in here, that's it.

434
00:27:55,900 --> 00:27:57,890
I might as well be back in a war zone.

435
00:27:57,940 --> 00:28:01,609
- So you'll just let Rigsy die?
- To keep the peace? Yeah, I will.

436
00:28:01,660 --> 00:28:05,700
Your friend, acting like he was all
scared of us, calling for a doctor.

437
00:28:06,700 --> 00:28:09,569
- A what?
- I know. The cheek of it!

438
00:28:09,619 --> 00:28:11,730
Humans can survive losing entire limbs

439
00:28:11,779 --> 00:28:14,210
- and I'm supposed to believe he...
- Shut up! Shut up.

440
00:28:14,259 --> 00:28:16,569
The other thing you said,
the second thing... what,

441
00:28:16,619 --> 00:28:17,970
you said he was scared and...

442
00:28:18,019 --> 00:28:21,250
And he asked the Mayor
to call HIM a doctor.

443
00:28:21,299 --> 00:28:24,569
- Poor Anah, dead at his feet, and...
- Shh!

444
00:28:24,619 --> 00:28:27,049
Did he say "a doctor" or "the doctor"?

445
00:28:27,099 --> 00:28:28,930
This is very, very important.

446
00:28:28,980 --> 00:28:31,769
THE doctor. There was nothing
wrong with him, mind.

447
00:28:31,819 --> 00:28:34,819
It was all just your
standard human lies...

448
00:28:40,140 --> 00:28:42,769
Clara gave you my number for emergencies.

449
00:28:42,819 --> 00:28:45,089
So when you wake up with a
weird tattoo on your neck

450
00:28:45,140 --> 00:28:46,890
and no memory of the last 24 hours

451
00:28:46,940 --> 00:28:49,210
the first thing you do is call the Doctor.

452
00:28:49,259 --> 00:28:50,650
"Call the Doctor."

453
00:28:50,700 --> 00:28:54,130
But you find yourself accused of
murder on a strange alien street

454
00:28:54,180 --> 00:28:56,730
in the middle of London?
Only they've taken your phone

455
00:28:56,779 --> 00:28:59,410
so you beg the woman in
charge to call me instead.

456
00:28:59,460 --> 00:29:03,609
She knew you and I were
friends. So why did she lie?

457
00:29:03,660 --> 00:29:06,859
- Unless she had something to hide.
- Murderer!

458
00:29:07,980 --> 00:29:11,569
There's something very wrong here
and we're running out of time.

459
00:29:11,619 --> 00:29:14,809
There's 12 minutes left.
I'm not giving up yet.

460
00:29:14,859 --> 00:29:17,690
Clara, even if one of
them knows something,

461
00:29:17,740 --> 00:29:20,900
they're not going to come
forward. The way they look at me...

462
00:29:22,299 --> 00:29:23,890
The way they look at you?

463
00:29:23,940 --> 00:29:25,380
What?

464
00:29:31,380 --> 00:29:32,849
Hey. Wait.

465
00:29:34,500 --> 00:29:38,500
Everyone here is weird around us
because of Rigsy. But not you.

466
00:29:40,099 --> 00:29:42,970
You look at me and the
Doctor like you're confused.

467
00:29:43,019 --> 00:29:44,170
Like you're curious.

468
00:29:44,220 --> 00:29:47,289
- I don't know what you mean.
- You do.

469
00:29:47,339 --> 00:29:49,329
You know Rigsy is innocent

470
00:29:49,380 --> 00:29:52,809
because you can look into
his past and you can see it.

471
00:29:52,859 --> 00:29:54,339
Can't you?

472
00:29:59,539 --> 00:30:04,299
She dressed you as a boy to protect
you, but really you're a girl.

473
00:30:05,900 --> 00:30:07,490
You have the gift.

474
00:30:07,539 --> 00:30:11,380
It is no gift. I'm safe as a boy.

475
00:30:12,779 --> 00:30:15,210
This is the first place
I've ever been safe,

476
00:30:15,259 --> 00:30:17,730
and you want me to throw it away?

477
00:30:17,779 --> 00:30:19,250
To admit what I am?

478
00:30:19,299 --> 00:30:21,900
The Mayor... what is she up to?

479
00:30:23,019 --> 00:30:25,160
It's nothing good, is it?

480
00:30:25,579 --> 00:30:27,720
I can't see everything...

481
00:30:29,099 --> 00:30:32,089
but she thinks she's
doing the right thing.

482
00:30:32,140 --> 00:30:33,529
They usually do.

483
00:30:33,579 --> 00:30:35,769
If what Ashildr is doing is harmless,

484
00:30:35,819 --> 00:30:37,970
then we'll just walk out of that door.

485
00:30:38,019 --> 00:30:41,730
No-one will know of your
abilities. But if it's not...

486
00:30:41,779 --> 00:30:44,809
I don't know what she means to do.

487
00:30:44,859 --> 00:30:47,460
No, I'm trying! But I can't see it.

488
00:30:48,259 --> 00:30:51,289
I can't see it because it involves you.

489
00:30:51,339 --> 00:30:55,450
When I look at you, I can't
tell your past from your future,

490
00:30:55,500 --> 00:30:57,970
and there's so very much of both.

491
00:30:59,380 --> 00:31:02,579
This isn't about Rigsy. It's about me.

492
00:31:11,220 --> 00:31:13,660
She couldn't just ask you here.

493
00:31:15,539 --> 00:31:17,170
She needed a mystery.

494
00:31:17,220 --> 00:31:21,130
You can never resist a mystery.

495
00:31:21,180 --> 00:31:22,849
She's afraid.

496
00:31:22,900 --> 00:31:25,779
Afraid of what? Of whom?

497
00:31:27,099 --> 00:31:28,640
I can't see.

498
00:31:35,900 --> 00:31:37,369
I'm sorry.

499
00:31:44,500 --> 00:31:47,900
You! Hold your tongue.
We've got ten minutes left!

500
00:31:49,180 --> 00:31:52,650
Ashildr said Anah was being
taken home for burial.

501
00:31:52,700 --> 00:31:54,849
But the Janus burn their dead.

502
00:31:54,900 --> 00:31:56,500
Is that true?

503
00:31:58,099 --> 00:32:02,009
So, look, Ashildr got it wrong.
What does it matter? Come on!

504
00:32:02,059 --> 00:32:06,559
- There's something about this tech...
- Doctor, we don't have time!

505
00:32:06,740 --> 00:32:07,769
What is it?

506
00:32:07,819 --> 00:32:10,970
It looks like medical data.

507
00:32:11,019 --> 00:32:14,420
But it can't be. She's dead.
She isn't breathing.

508
00:32:14,980 --> 00:32:17,049
This thing is a stasis pod.

509
00:32:17,099 --> 00:32:20,609
If you're dead, it's a
kind of fancy refrigerator.

510
00:32:20,660 --> 00:32:22,660
But if you're alive...

511
00:32:23,500 --> 00:32:25,529
it simply keeps you that way.

512
00:32:25,579 --> 00:32:26,930
She's alive?

513
00:32:26,980 --> 00:32:28,450
She's alive.

514
00:32:28,500 --> 00:32:30,450
Well, get her out! Get her out of there!

515
00:32:30,500 --> 00:32:32,410
There must be a way to unlock it...

516
00:32:32,460 --> 00:32:35,130
- something basic, something simple I'm missing.
- A keyhole!

517
00:32:35,180 --> 00:32:39,849
- A keyhole would be very handy, yes, but...
- No, no, a keyhole! Look!

518
00:32:39,900 --> 00:32:42,009
I'll find her. I'll get the key.

519
00:32:42,059 --> 00:32:44,130
No, Anahson, stay here.

520
00:32:45,619 --> 00:32:48,579
There's a reason the Mayor's gone AWOL.

521
00:32:49,700 --> 00:32:51,690
She means for us to release your mother,

522
00:32:51,740 --> 00:32:54,049
but she doesn't want us to use her key.

523
00:32:54,099 --> 00:32:56,210
She wants to use mine.

524
00:32:56,259 --> 00:32:59,000
The TARDIS? That's what this is about?

525
00:33:02,099 --> 00:33:03,369
Doctor, wait!

526
00:33:03,420 --> 00:33:05,619
This girl needs her mother.

527
00:33:05,819 --> 00:33:06,890
Doctor!

528
00:33:06,940 --> 00:33:08,690
I can't...

529
00:33:08,740 --> 00:33:10,980
What's it doing?

530
00:33:16,859 --> 00:33:18,059
Mum!

531
00:33:19,220 --> 00:33:20,690
Mum? Are you OK?

532
00:33:20,740 --> 00:33:23,329
She'll be perfectly fine in a few moments.

533
00:33:23,380 --> 00:33:24,650
I assure you.

534
00:33:24,700 --> 00:33:27,130
There are easier ways to
steal a key, you know.

535
00:33:27,180 --> 00:33:31,140
I don't want your TARDIS.
That's not what this is about.

536
00:33:32,500 --> 00:33:34,609
Rigsy, come here, I'll
remove your chronolock.

537
00:33:34,660 --> 00:33:37,569
What is this, Ashildr? You can't
possibly think it'll keep me here.

538
00:33:37,619 --> 00:33:39,930
It's not a restraint.

539
00:33:39,980 --> 00:33:42,210
- It's a teleport bracelet.
- What?

540
00:33:42,259 --> 00:33:45,809
I'll give you time to say goodbye.
Don't worry. No-one will be hurt.

541
00:33:45,859 --> 00:33:48,000
Where are you sending me?

542
00:33:48,980 --> 00:33:51,579
I made a deal to protect the street.

543
00:33:52,140 --> 00:33:55,170
They take you, I take the
key so you can't be traced.

544
00:33:55,220 --> 00:33:57,849
I do as they tell me,
and the street is safe.

545
00:33:57,900 --> 00:34:00,289
They? Who are "they?"

546
00:34:00,339 --> 00:34:02,009
One more thing.

547
00:34:03,619 --> 00:34:05,559
Your confession dial.

548
00:34:07,259 --> 00:34:09,969
They have other means of procuring it,

549
00:34:10,019 --> 00:34:13,420
but I understand it's
likely to be on your person.

550
00:34:14,380 --> 00:34:17,650
Please, no resistance.

551
00:34:17,699 --> 00:34:19,639
You've already lost.

552
00:34:25,420 --> 00:34:26,849
What is it?

553
00:34:26,900 --> 00:34:28,889
In your terms, my last will and testament.

554
00:34:28,940 --> 00:34:31,820
- How does it work?
- I've no idea.

555
00:34:33,139 --> 00:34:35,210
Well, thank you anyway.

556
00:34:36,019 --> 00:34:38,769
Rigsy, your neck.

557
00:34:38,820 --> 00:34:42,019
Clara, what are you playing
at? The chronolock!

558
00:34:43,380 --> 00:34:45,610
Take the teleport off him first!

559
00:34:45,659 --> 00:34:48,659
I don't have it, I'm
telling you. Clara does!

560
00:34:52,780 --> 00:34:54,929
No...

561
00:34:54,980 --> 00:34:56,650
No, you didn't.

562
00:34:57,699 --> 00:34:59,889
Go on, then. Take it off.

563
00:34:59,940 --> 00:35:01,880
Clara, you didn't...!

564
00:35:05,860 --> 00:35:08,860
I had no idea she'd do
something so stupid.

565
00:35:10,739 --> 00:35:14,219
I swear, I never meant
for anyone to get hurt.

566
00:35:15,699 --> 00:35:18,889
What were you thinking,
sacrificing yourself?

567
00:35:18,940 --> 00:35:20,730
I wasn't sacrificing anything!

568
00:35:20,780 --> 00:35:23,769
It was strategy. Backup
plan, to buy us more time.

569
00:35:23,820 --> 00:35:26,889
- Who told you to give it to her?
- Nobody did! I did! Rump said...

570
00:35:26,940 --> 00:35:29,409
- What exactly did Rump say?
- He said the death is locked in.

571
00:35:29,460 --> 00:35:32,010
You can pass it on, but...

572
00:35:32,059 --> 00:35:33,460
But what?

573
00:35:36,659 --> 00:35:38,250
But...

574
00:35:38,300 --> 00:35:40,840
But you can't cheat it altogether.

575
00:35:50,739 --> 00:35:53,650
Clara, you didn't tell me that!

576
00:35:53,699 --> 00:35:55,210
Give it back to me, now.

577
00:35:55,260 --> 00:35:56,730
She can't.

578
00:35:59,699 --> 00:36:01,610
Clara, I made a contract with the Shade

579
00:36:01,659 --> 00:36:04,289
when I put the chronolock on Rigsy.

580
00:36:04,340 --> 00:36:07,329
I promised it a soul and only
I can break that contract.

581
00:36:07,380 --> 00:36:10,449
When you took it from
him, you changed the terms.

582
00:36:10,500 --> 00:36:12,699
You cut me out of the deal.

583
00:36:28,980 --> 00:36:31,179
We can fix this. Can't we?

584
00:36:35,579 --> 00:36:37,380
We always fix it.

585
00:36:39,860 --> 00:36:41,099
No.

586
00:36:47,619 --> 00:36:48,849
But you can.

587
00:36:48,900 --> 00:36:50,610
Fix this. Fix it now.

588
00:36:50,659 --> 00:36:53,929
It... It's not possible. I can't.

589
00:36:53,980 --> 00:36:58,289
Yes, it is, you can, and you will,
or this street will be over.

590
00:36:58,340 --> 00:37:02,369
I'll show you and all your funny little
friends to the whole laughing world.

591
00:37:02,420 --> 00:37:04,250
I'll bring UNIT, I'll bring the Zygons.

592
00:37:04,300 --> 00:37:06,969
Give me a minute, I'll bring
the Daleks and the Cybermen.

593
00:37:07,019 --> 00:37:09,809
You will save Clara,
and you will do it now,

594
00:37:09,860 --> 00:37:12,489
or I will rain hell on
you for the rest of time.

595
00:37:12,539 --> 00:37:13,969
Doctor, stop talking like that.

596
00:37:14,019 --> 00:37:16,969
- You can't.
- I can do whatever the hell I like.

597
00:37:17,019 --> 00:37:19,610
You've read the stories,
you know who I am.

598
00:37:19,659 --> 00:37:21,489
And in all of that time,

599
00:37:21,539 --> 00:37:25,010
did you ever hear anything
about anyone who stopped me?

600
00:37:25,059 --> 00:37:27,530
I know the Doctor. The
Doctor would never...

601
00:37:27,579 --> 00:37:30,579
The Doctor is no longer
here! You are stuck with me.

602
00:37:31,619 --> 00:37:34,289
And I will end you, and
everything you love.

603
00:37:34,340 --> 00:37:37,409
- Doctor, for God's sake, will you stop?
- No!

604
00:37:37,460 --> 00:37:41,289
I did this, do you hear me? I
did this. This was my fault.

605
00:37:41,340 --> 00:37:45,139
- I don't care.
- Liar. You always care. Always have.

606
00:37:46,579 --> 00:37:48,010
Your reign of terror will end

607
00:37:48,059 --> 00:37:51,460
- with the sight of the first crying child and you know it.
- No, I don't.

608
00:37:53,579 --> 00:37:54,980
I do.

609
00:38:03,619 --> 00:38:08,500
Listen... If this is the last
I ever see of you, please...

610
00:38:10,460 --> 00:38:12,260
not like this.

611
00:38:24,099 --> 00:38:26,440
Is there anything you can do?

612
00:38:27,300 --> 00:38:30,690
I'm sorry, I'm truly sorry, I...

613
00:38:30,739 --> 00:38:33,019
Time's short. Yes or no?

614
00:38:34,219 --> 00:38:35,360
No.

615
00:38:49,219 --> 00:38:51,489
- Well, if Danny Pink can do it, so can I.
- Do what?

616
00:38:51,539 --> 00:38:53,769
Die right. Die like I
mean it. Face the raven.

617
00:38:53,820 --> 00:38:56,170
No, this isn't happening,
this can't be happening.

618
00:38:56,219 --> 00:38:57,650
Maybe this is what I wanted.

619
00:38:57,699 --> 00:39:00,329
Maybe this is it. Maybe
this is why I kept running.

620
00:39:00,380 --> 00:39:02,889
Maybe this is why I kept
taking all those stupid risks.

621
00:39:02,940 --> 00:39:04,730
- Kept pushing it.
- This is my fault.

622
00:39:04,780 --> 00:39:06,889
- This is my choice.
- I let you get reckless.

623
00:39:06,940 --> 00:39:08,650
Why? Why shouldn't I be so reckless?

624
00:39:08,699 --> 00:39:12,090
You're reckless all the bloody
time! Why can't I be like you?

625
00:39:12,139 --> 00:39:14,449
Clara, there's nothing
special about me. I'm nothing

626
00:39:14,500 --> 00:39:17,210
but I'm less breakable than you.
I should have taken care of you.

627
00:39:17,260 --> 00:39:20,760
- I never asked you to.
- You shouldn't have to ask.

628
00:39:22,420 --> 00:39:24,730
- Clara, if I'd known, I'd...
- Don't. Shut up.

629
00:39:24,780 --> 00:39:27,409
- But...
- Really, Rigsy, shut up.

630
00:39:27,460 --> 00:39:31,260
If you feel guilty about this,
even for one minute, I...

631
00:39:40,420 --> 00:39:41,900
You.

632
00:39:44,500 --> 00:39:46,500
Now, you listen to me.

633
00:39:48,539 --> 00:39:52,090
You're going to be alone now,
and you're very bad at that.

634
00:39:52,139 --> 00:39:55,409
You're going to be furious
and you're going to be sad,

635
00:39:55,460 --> 00:39:58,010
but listen to me. Don't
let this change you.

636
00:39:58,059 --> 00:40:00,699
No, listen. Whatever happens next...

637
00:40:02,059 --> 00:40:05,780
wherever she is sending you,
I know what you're capable of.

638
00:40:07,139 --> 00:40:10,010
You don't be a warrior.

639
00:40:10,059 --> 00:40:11,849
Promise me.

640
00:40:11,900 --> 00:40:13,619
Be a Doctor.

641
00:40:14,860 --> 00:40:19,010
- What's the point of being a Doctor if I can't cure you?
- Heal yourself.

642
00:40:19,059 --> 00:40:23,420
You have to. You can't let this
turn you into a monster. So...

643
00:40:24,699 --> 00:40:27,980
I'm not asking you for a
promise. I'm giving you an order.

644
00:40:31,099 --> 00:40:32,969
You will not insult my memory.

645
00:40:33,019 --> 00:40:34,849
There will be no revenge.

646
00:40:34,900 --> 00:40:39,099
I will die, and no-one else,
here or anywhere, will suffer.

647
00:40:42,739 --> 00:40:44,409
What about me?

648
00:40:45,900 --> 00:40:49,500
If there was something I
could do about that, I would.

649
00:40:50,219 --> 00:40:53,619
I guess we're both just
going to have to be brave.

650
00:40:57,340 --> 00:40:58,739
Clara...

651
00:41:04,300 --> 00:41:07,539
Everything you are about
to say, I already know.

652
00:41:09,500 --> 00:41:13,500
Don't do it now. We've
already had enough bad timing.

653
00:41:20,780 --> 00:41:22,500
Don't run.

654
00:41:25,539 --> 00:41:27,139
Stay with me.

655
00:41:30,179 --> 00:41:31,739
Nah.

656
00:41:33,300 --> 00:41:34,969
You stay here.

657
00:41:35,940 --> 00:41:38,489
In the end, everybody does this alone.

658
00:41:38,539 --> 00:41:43,539
- Clara...
- This is as brave... as I know how to be.

659
00:41:47,420 --> 00:41:50,619
I know it's going to hurt
you, but... please...

660
00:41:52,019 --> 00:41:54,219
be a little proud of me.

661
00:42:09,059 --> 00:42:10,800
Goodbye, Doctor.

662
00:42:57,139 --> 00:42:59,530
Let me be brave.

663
00:42:59,579 --> 00:43:01,380
Let me be brave...

664
00:44:27,340 --> 00:44:29,139
I'm sorry, Doctor.

665
00:44:30,059 --> 00:44:31,530
I truly am.

666
00:44:46,619 --> 00:44:49,329
What Clara said about
not taking revenge...

667
00:44:49,380 --> 00:44:51,719
Do you know why she said that?

668
00:44:52,059 --> 00:44:54,849
- She was saving you.
- I was lost a long time ago.

669
00:44:54,900 --> 00:44:56,769
She was saving you.

670
00:45:00,460 --> 00:45:05,539
I'll do my best. But I strongly
advise you to keep out of my way.

671
00:45:08,019 --> 00:45:11,739
You'll find it's a very small
universe when I'm angry with you.

672
00:46:27,980 --> 00:46:30,730
This whole place is
designed to terrify me.

673
00:46:30,780 --> 00:46:33,780
I know you. I've seen you before.

674
00:46:34,860 --> 00:46:37,889
If you were any part in killing
her and you're not afraid,

675
00:46:37,940 --> 00:46:40,210
then you understand nothing at all.

676
00:46:40,260 --> 00:46:43,610
I am the Doctor, I'm coming to find you

677
00:46:43,659 --> 00:46:45,739
and I will never, ever stop.
